// ora-arch-fin — generated from src/ora-arch-fin-*.js by tools/merge.js. Edit the src files, then run: node tools/merge.js
// Oracle Senior Solution Architect (10–15 yrs) — Finance. Format: [module, question, answer, 'B' basic | 'D' in-depth]
QB["ora-arch-fin"] = (QB["ora-arch-fin"] || []).concat([
["Architecture", "What does a Senior Solution Architect own on an Oracle Finance program that a lead consultant doesn't?", "**Why they ask:** to see if you understand the step up from lead to architect.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"A lead owns a workstream; an architect owns the whole solution. That means end-to-end design across modules, EPM, integrations, data, security and reporting. It means architecture decisions with documented trade-offs and their long-term cost. It means non-functional requirements: performance, volumes, availability, security, auditability and upgradeability. It means the integration and data architecture, including which platform and who owns master data. I run the design authority that reviews workstream designs for consistency. I make sure today's design fits the roadmap: acquisitions, new countries and AI. And I explain options and risk to the CFO and CIO.\"\n\nTip: Open with \"a lead owns a workstream, an architect owns the whole\". It frames everything else.", "B"],
["Architecture", "How do you document and govern architecture decisions?", "**Why they ask:** undocumented decisions get reopened and forgotten.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I use Architecture Decision Records: the context, options considered, the decision, consequences, owner and date. A design authority meets weekly, and workstreams bring any decision that affects others. We agree principles up front, like 'adopt standard first', 'one integration platform', 'no direct database access' and who owns which master data. Requirements trace to design, configuration and tests. And decisions are revisited when their assumptions change.\"\n\nTip: Give one principle you've used. It shows the governance is real.", "B"],
["Enterprise Structures", "Design the enterprise structure for a company with 40 legal entities in 15 countries, a US parent reporting in USD under US GAAP.", "**Why they ask:** a senior architecture question. They want clear choices and trade-offs.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"For ledgers, I'd create primary ledgers per country and currency, using one global chart of accounts where possible. For each non-USD ledger, I'd add a USD reporting currency if the business needs daily USD visibility; otherwise translation in consolidation is enough. Secondary ledgers only where local GAAP differs materially and must be kept in Oracle; otherwise local adjustments go in a statutory tool. Ledger sets by region make period management easier. Legal entities get balancing segment values; business units go by country or region, with shared services BUs providing AP and AR. FCCS handles group consolidation, eliminations and minority interests. The trade-offs I'd walk the client through are a global vs local chart, the number of ledgers vs close effort, and the translation approach.\"\n\nTip: State the trade-offs at the end. Architects are judged on how they explain choices.", "D"],
["Enterprise Structures", "When would you put multiple legal entities in one ledger and when would you separate them?", "**Why they ask:** a key design decision in every Fusion project.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Entities can share a ledger when they share the four Cs: chart of accounts, calendar, currency and accounting method. Then each entity gets its own balancing segment values. That means fewer ledgers to close, simpler intercompany and shared reporting. I'd separate them when any of the four Cs differ, or when statutory reporting or local auditors need strict separation, when close timelines differ, when security is easier at ledger level, or when a divestiture is planned and a clean carve-out matters. The trade-off with many entities in one ledger is that intercompany balancing rules and security by balancing segment need careful design.\"\n\nTip: Mention the divestiture case. It shows you think beyond the setup screen.", "D"],
["Integration", "How do you choose between REST APIs, FBDI, SOAP and business events for Finance integrations?", "**Why they ask:** picking the wrong pattern causes performance and support problems.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"REST is for real-time, low-to-medium volume, record-level work, like creating a supplier or checking an invoice status, within rate limits and payload sizes. FBDI is for high-volume batch loads like journals, invoices and AutoInvoice, run asynchronously through the ERP Integration Service with callbacks. SOAP is for objects that only have SOAP services, or legacy integrations. Business events are outbound and event-driven, like invoice approved or PO approved, for near real-time updates downstream. And BICC or BIP extracts push bulk data out to a data lake or other systems. My rule: events for real time, FBDI for bulk inbound, BICC for bulk outbound, REST for transactional lookups.\"\n\nTip: State the rule at the end. It's what architects are expected to have.", "D"],
["Integration", "How would you design an integration architecture for Oracle Fusion with 50+ systems?", "**Why they ask:** a classic architecture question.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"One integration platform, OIC or an enterprise iPaaS like MuleSoft, never point-to-point. Standard patterns: event-driven, scheduled batch, and API-led with system, process and experience APIs. Master data flows with a clear source of truth for each domain, like HCM for workers, Fusion for suppliers and CRM for customers. Central error handling with logging, retries, dead-letter queues, alerts and a reprocessing screen. Security with OAuth, least-privilege integration users and secrets in a vault. Monitoring dashboards with SLAs per integration. CI/CD and regression tests on every quarterly update. And an inventory of every integration with an owner.\"\n\nTip: Mention a reprocessing screen for the business. It reduces support tickets to IT.", "D"],
["Integration", "How do you design a high-volume journal integration from a banking or billing system?", "**Why they ask:** high-volume journals are a common performance trap.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I summarize at the source where possible, by account and day, because GL isn't a transaction store; detail stays in a subledger or Fusion Accounting Hub. I use FBDI journal import with chunking, say 100,000 lines per file, and parallel imports by ledger. Account combinations are validated before sending, using cross-reference tables. Unique batch IDs prevent double loading. Each batch has control totals, and an automated comparison checks source against GL. Loads are scheduled around close and monitored. And if the business needs detailed accounting rules for that source, I'd use Accounting Hub instead.\"\n\nTip: Say \"GL isn't a transaction store\". It's the key architectural point.", "D"],
["Accounting Hub", "What is Fusion Accounting Hub (FAH) and when would you use it?", "**Why they ask:** to see if you know when to bring in Accounting Hub.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Accounting Hub lets you create subledger accounting from non-Oracle systems, like banking, insurance policy administration, billing or loans, using the same SLA rules engine Fusion uses. You define a subledger application, an event model, sources and accounting rules, load transactions through FBDI or REST, and run Create Accounting to produce journals in GL with drill-down to the source details. It supports more than one GAAP, for example IFRS 17 and US GAAP, through separate accounting methods. I'd use it when the source system can't produce good accounting, or when finance needs to control the rules in one place.\"\n\nTip: Give an industry example, like insurance or banking. It anchors the answer.", "D"],
["Data Architecture", "How would you design analytics and data extraction from Fusion Financials?", "**Why they ask:** analytics design affects production performance.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Fusion Data Intelligence gives a prebuilt warehouse with finance KPIs on Autonomous Data Warehouse, and it can be extended with other data. BICC incremental extracts go to object storage or a data lake for enterprise platforms like Snowflake or Databricks. OTBI and BIP handle operational reporting inside the app. EPM handles planning and management reporting. Governance covers data definitions, like what 'spend' means, reconciliation of the warehouse to GL, and replicating security. I avoid heavy BIP extracts for analytics, because they load production and are hard to maintain.\"\n\nTip: End with the BIP warning. It shows experience.", "D"],
["Security", "How do you architect security for a global Finance Cloud deployment?", "**Why they ask:** security design is an architect's responsibility.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Roles are designed around job families, by copying and trimming seeded roles and keeping custom privileges to a minimum. Data security uses BUs, ledgers and data access sets, with segment value security only where it's essential. An SoD rule set in Advanced Access Controls or a GRC tool checks at provisioning. Provisioning is automated from HCM with role mapping rules, and IAM gives SSO through SAML or OIDC with MFA. Privileged access for admins and implementers is time-limited and logged. Audit policies cover sensitive setups, with quarterly access reviews. And integration users get least privilege.\"\n\nTip: Mention preventive SoD checks at provisioning. Catching conflicts before access is granted is better than after.", "D"],
["Environments", "How do you plan the environment (pod) strategy for an Oracle Cloud program?", "**Why they ask:** environment planning avoids conflicts and delays.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Typically production, test or UAT, development or CRP, and sometimes extra ones for data migration or training. I plan production-to-test and test-to-test refreshes, and after go-live I refresh test from production regularly for realistic testing. Configuration moves with configuration packages, or manually with checklists, and extensions through proper deployment. Test environments get quarterly updates two weeks early. Sensitive data is masked in non-production. And an environment calendar prevents conflicts between projects.\"\n\nTip: Mention data masking after refreshes. It's often forgotten and it's a privacy risk.", "B"],
["Extensions", "What extension options exist for Oracle Fusion and how do you decide?", "**Why they ask:** extension choices affect upgrades for years.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Configuration first: flexfields, lookups, rules, approval workflows and personalization. Then Visual Builder and Redwood extensions for UI on Fusion data through REST. OIC for integrations and process automation. BIP and OTBI for reports. Application Composer and Groovy in some areas. OCI services, like Functions or APEX on Autonomous Database, for side applications. And AI Agent Studio for agentic automation. I decide by business value, upgrade safety using only supported APIs, the skills to maintain it, and security. And I keep an extension inventory that's reviewed every year.\"\n\nTip: Mention the yearly inventory review. Unused extensions are hidden cost.", "D"],
["Migration", "How would you architect a phased EBS-to-Fusion migration with coexistence?", "**Why they ask:** most large migrations are phased.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I'd phase by module, Financials first and SCM later, or by entity or region. Coexistence integrations connect the two: EBS SCM receipts and POs to Fusion AP for matching, EBS OM to Fusion AR, and EBS inventory accounting to Fusion GL as summary journals. Master data has one source during coexistence, for example suppliers mastered in Fusion and synced to EBS. A data warehouse supports reporting across both. Coexistence integrations are expensive, so I retire them quickly. And historical data goes to an archive with reporting.\"\n\nTip: Say \"retire coexistence quickly\". It's the cost people underestimate.", "D"],
["M&A", "How would you integrate an acquired company into the Oracle Fusion Finance platform?", "**Why they ask:** M&A readiness is a strategic platform quality.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"On day one, I'd consolidate their trial balance into FCCS through a mapping, with no system change. Then assess their processes, systems, chart of accounts, entities and contracts. Onboarding uses the global template: new legal entities, a ledger or balancing values, and BUs, plus localizations. Data migration covers open AP and AR, assets and balances at a month-end. Integrations cover their banks, payroll and operational systems. People and controls cover roles, SoD and SOX scope. And I'd make the platform M&A-ready with a documented template, automation scripts and a repeatable playbook, targeting something like 90 days to onboard.\"\n\nTip: Mention the 90-day playbook. It shows you build for repeatability.", "D"],
["M&A", "How would you support a divestiture (carve-out) of a business unit in Oracle?", "**Why they ask:** divestitures are hard if the design didn't plan for them.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"First I identify everything belonging to the business: ledgers or balancing values, BUs, suppliers and customers, assets, open transactions and users. Then the options: a transition service agreement, where we run processes for the buyer for a period; a clone of the environment for the buyer; or a data extract for the buyer's system. Data access between us and the divested business is restricted, which is often a legal requirement. Cut-off dates for open items and intercompany settlement are planned. And the lesson for design is to keep separate ledgers or balancing values per entity from the start, which makes carve-outs much easier.\"\n\nTip: End with the design lesson. It shows foresight.", "D"],
["Banking", "Design the bank integration architecture for a multinational using Oracle Fusion.", "**Why they ask:** an architect question. They want options, standards, security and monitoring.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"There are three ways to connect: host-to-host with each bank, SWIFT through Alliance Lite2 or a service bureau, or a bank connectivity hub. For 15 or more banks I'd lean toward a hub, because each new bank is then a configuration, not a project. I'd standardize on ISO 20022: pain.001 for payments, pain.002 for status, and CAMT.053 and CAMT.052 for statements. Security is PGP encryption and signing, managed SFTP keys, and separation of who releases payments. The flow is: payment process requests create files, the hub sends them to banks, and acknowledgments and statements come back into Cash Management. If treasury needs an in-house bank or netting, that lives in a TMS integrated with Fusion. And I'd monitor every transmission and acknowledgment, with alerts when a file doesn't arrive.\"\n\nTip: Recommend one option and say why. Architects are expected to choose.", "D"],
["Tax", "How would you architect indirect tax for a global Fusion implementation?", "**Why they ask:** a senior design question across many countries.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I'd use Fusion Tax natively for VAT and GST countries, where rules-based setup works well, and a partner engine like Vertex, Avalara or Sovos for US sales and use tax, and possibly worldwide if the tax team prefers one engine. E-invoicing mandates, like Italy SDI, Mexico CFDI, India e-invoicing, PEPPOL and EU ViDA, go through Oracle localizations or partners. Tax returns and SAF-T come from the tax reporting ledger or the partner. Master data matters: party tax profiles, registrations and product tax classifications. And governance: the tax team owns the rules, changes go through change control, and every rate change gets a regression test.\"\n\nTip: Separate US sales tax from VAT. It shows you understand why most companies use a partner for the US.", "D"],
["Non-Functional", "What non-functional requirements matter for a Finance Cloud design and how do you address them?", "**Why they ask:** NFRs are an architect's job.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Performance: volumes like invoices per day, journal lines and AutoInvoice lines, batch windows and close processes, tested with production-like volumes. Availability: Oracle provides the SaaS SLA, so I plan for integration outages rather than the core app. Security and compliance: SOX, GDPR with data residency and personal data in supplier and customer records, and no card data for PCI. Auditability: audit trails and log retention. Scalability for new entities and acquisitions. And maintainability: standard features, documented extensions and regression automation.\"\n\nTip: Mention testing with production-like volumes. It's the NFR most often skipped.", "B"],
["Performance", "A Create Accounting job runs for 6 hours at month end. How do you approach it?", "**Why they ask:** a performance troubleshooting question.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"First I check volumes and timing: is everything being accounted at month-end? If so, move to daily accounting. Then parallelize by ledger or application and set the processing parameters properly. Then review SLA rules, because complex custom sources, heavy mapping sets or many supporting references slow things down. I check for errors causing reprocessing, and for other jobs, like AutoInvoice or big reports, competing at the same time. If it's still abnormal, I raise an SR with Oracle with diagnostics. And I measure after each change.\"\n\nTip: Lead with \"move to daily accounting\". It usually solves most of the problem.", "D"],
["Reporting", "How do you architect management reporting vs statutory reporting?", "**Why they ask:** these two needs often conflict.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Statutory and GAAP reporting comes from GL, and FCCS for consolidated, and it's controlled, reconciled and audited. Management reporting often needs different hierarchies, allocations and non-GL data like headcount and volumes, so it goes to EPM, Planning or Profitability and Cost Management, or Fusion Data Intelligence. One chart of accounts with multiple hierarchies supports both. GL stays the single source of truth for actuals, and any warehouse reconciles to it. Narrative Reporting builds board and statutory packs.\"\n\nTip: Say \"one COA, many hierarchies\". It's the design that serves both.", "D"],
["EPM", "How do Oracle EPM modules fit with Fusion Financials?", "**Why they ask:** finance architects need to see the whole Oracle finance stack.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Planning handles budgets and forecasts, and approved budgets can go to GL for budgetary control or reporting. FCCS handles consolidation and close. Account Reconciliation handles balance sheet reconciliations and transaction matching. Narrative Reporting builds management and board packages. Tax Reporting does the income tax provision. Profitability and Cost Management handles allocations and profitability. They connect to Fusion through Data Integration, sending GL balances to EPM with drill-back to Fusion. The key is designing accounts and entities consistently across GL and EPM.\"\n\nTip: End with consistent dimensions. It's the architect's real job here.", "D"],
["AI", "How would you architect AI agents in an Oracle Finance landscape responsibly?", "**Why they ask:** finance leaders want AI, but not at the cost of control.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I'd build agents in Oracle AI Agent Studio, because they run on Fusion data with Fusion security. I'd pick use cases with clear value, like invoice exceptions, collections emails, reconciliation explanations and close insights. Guardrails: the agent acts only within the user's or a defined service role's access, and anything that posts to the ledger or pays money needs human approval. Every prompt, action and decision is logged for audit. No sensitive data goes to an outside model without approval. I'd measure accuracy, time saved and exceptions, and support finance users through the change.\"\n\nTip: Say \"human approval for postings and payments\" early. It's what the controller is listening for.", "D"],
["Stakeholders", "How do you present architecture options to a CFO and CIO?", "**Why they ask:** architects must communicate with executives.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I start with the business question and the decision needed. Then two or three options, each with build and run cost, risk, time, business impact and future flexibility. Then a clear recommendation and why. One simple diagram, no jargon. And what happens if we don't decide now. For example: 'Separate ledgers per country add two hours to close but make local statutory reporting and divestitures easier. I recommend them only for countries with different fiscal years.'\"\n\nTip: Always give a recommendation. Executives want your view, not just options.", "B"],
["Behavioral", "Describe an architecture decision you made that you later regretted. What did you learn?", "**Why they ask:** honesty and learning.\n\n**Answer (example):**\n\"To save time, I allowed a custom BIP extract to feed a downstream data warehouse. As volumes grew, it slowed production at peak times, and it broke after a quarterly update. We moved it to BICC incremental extracts to object storage. What I learned: use the platform's intended pattern even if it takes longer up front, and think about growth and upgrades in every decision.\"\n\nTip: Own it and give a lasting lesson. That's all they want.", "B"]
]);

QB["ora-arch-fin"] = (QB["ora-arch-fin"] || []).concat([
["Chart of Accounts", "A client wants to add a new segment to the COA two years after go-live. How do you respond?", "**Why they ask:** a real consulting situation. They want you to push back with options, not just say no.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"First I'd ask what reporting is missing, because often it can be done without a new segment: hierarchies, supporting references, descriptive flexfields, projects or an EPM dimension. If a segment really is needed, Fusion doesn't let you just add a segment to a chart that's in use. The options are a new chart and new ledgers with a COA mapping and balance migration, or using a spare future segment if one was designed in. The impact is wide: integrations, SLA rules, reports, EPM, security, cross-validation rules and training. So I'd recommend a business case and doing it at a fiscal year boundary. And the lesson for every new design is to include a future segment.\"\n\nTip: Lead with \"what reporting is missing?\" It shows you solve the need, not the request.", "D"],
["Chart of Accounts", "What makes a chart of accounts \"future-proof\"?", "**Why they ask:** COA design is a long-term decision.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Each segment has one clear purpose, no overloaded segments. Value ranges have room to grow, and there's at least one future segment with a default value. Reporting uses hierarchies instead of hard-coded roll-ups. There's an intercompany segment from day one. Values are consistent globally, with controlled local extensions. There's governance for adding values: owners, approval and naming rules. And it lines up with EPM dimensions and consolidation.\"\n\nTip: Say \"one purpose per segment\". It's the rule that prevents most COA problems.", "B"],
["Subledger Accounting", "How would you design SLA for complex revenue and cost accounting across multiple GAAPs?", "**Why they ask:** a senior SLA design question.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I'd start by listing every event class that needs non-standard accounting. Then I'd copy the seeded method once per GAAP, say US GAAP and IFRS, and reuse as much as possible between them, like shared account derivation rules and mapping sets. The second GAAP runs in a secondary ledger with its own method at subledger level. I'd use custom sources sparingly and prefer seeded sources and descriptive flexfields. Supporting references, like contract ID or product, help reconciliation. Every rule gets documented with the business reason and tested with an accounting matrix. And governance is clear: finance owns the rules, IT owns deployment.\"\n\nTip: Mention the accounting matrix. It's the test document auditors and controllers want to see.", "D"],
["Intercompany", "How would you design an end-to-end intercompany solution?", "**Why they ask:** intercompany crosses procurement, finance, tax and treasury. They want the full picture.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I'd start by listing the types of flows: goods through supply chain financial orchestration, services and management fees through Fusion Intercompany, financing loans through treasury, and cost allocations through GL allocations with intercompany balancing. The chart has an intercompany segment and balancing rules by legal entity pair. For each country I decide whether intercompany needs a real invoice or just a journal, because tax and statutory rules often require invoices. Then settlement, usually monthly netting through treasury. Then reconciliation, with Fusion intercompany reconciliation or transaction matching in Account Reconciliation. Elimination happens in FCCS. And transfer pricing policy is agreed with tax.\"\n\nTip: Mention invoice vs journal-only by country. It's the decision that tax teams care about.", "D"],
["Period Close", "How would you architect a \"continuous close\" for a large enterprise?", "**Why they ask:** a forward-looking architect question.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"The idea is to do close work every day, not just at month-end. Subledgers account and post continuously, hourly or daily. Reconciliations are automated, with daily bank reconciliations and transaction matching in Account Reconciliation. Accruals and allocations run on schedules. Close tasks are managed in FCCS or Account Reconciliation with dependencies and dashboards. AI anomaly detection flags unusual journals and balances. Monthly is a soft close, with a hard close quarterly where it makes sense. And I'd track close KPIs and improve them every cycle.\"\n\nTip: Open with \"close work every day\". It explains the concept in one line.", "D"],
["Data Migration", "How do you architect data migration for a large Finance program?", "**Why they ask:** migration architecture at scale.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I set up a migration framework: extract, stage in a migration database, transform with mapping tables, validate, load with FBDI or REST, and reconcile. Loads and reconciliation scripts are automated so runs are repeatable. Data quality rules feed dashboards, and the business owns cleansing. Mocks are timed for cutover planning, and large loads are tuned. Each reconciliation and sign-off is kept as evidence. And the history strategy is summary balances in Fusion, with detail in an archive or warehouse.\"\n\nTip: Mention a staging database. It's what makes migrations repeatable.", "D"],
["Data Architecture", "How do you decide what historical data to bring into Fusion?", "**Why they ask:** history decisions drive cost and complexity.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I look at legal retention needs, which an archive usually meets better than the new ERP. Then business needs: prior-year comparatives, open items and asset history. Typically that means GL balances for one or two years by period in summary, open AP and AR, full asset records with accumulated depreciation, and open POs. Detailed history goes to an archive or warehouse with search and reporting. Each extra year of detail adds a lot of cost and complexity, so it's a business decision.\"\n\nTip: Say \"it's a business decision\". Architects frame it; the business owns it.", "B"],
["Integration", "How do you handle error management and reprocessing for Finance integrations?", "**Why they ask:** integration errors are where support time goes.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I sort errors into data errors, like a bad account; business errors, like a closed period; and technical errors, like timeouts. Technical errors retry with backoff. Business errors go to queues that finance users can see, with clear messages and owners, not only IT. After correction, they're reprocessed without duplicates, using idempotency keys. Alerts go out by severity, and there's a daily integration health report. And source and Fusion counts and totals are reconciled.\"\n\nTip: Say business errors should be visible to finance. It cuts support time in half.", "D"],
["Integration", "What is the role of business events and callbacks in Fusion integration design?", "**Why they ask:** event-driven design is the modern standard.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Business events, like Invoice Validated, Journal Batch Approved or Supplier Created, are published by Fusion and consumed by OIC subscriptions, so flows are event-driven without polling. Callbacks after bulk imports through the ERP Integration Service tell us when an FBDI job finishes, with status and logs, so the integration can continue or alert. The benefits are lower latency, less load and cleaner orchestration. I design for out-of-order and duplicate events.\"\n\nTip: Mention duplicate events. It's the trap in event-driven design.", "D"],
["Security", "How do you handle sensitive data (bank accounts, tax IDs) in a Fusion design?", "**Why they ask:** data protection is a design responsibility.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Bank account numbers are masked in the UI and reports for most roles, and only restricted roles can view or change sensitive data. Non-production environments are masked after every refresh. Data is encrypted in transit with TLS and PGP for files, and at rest by Oracle SaaS. Audit policies track changes. And for GDPR, personal data in supplier, customer and employee records has retention and erasure processes.\"\n\nTip: Mention masking after refreshes. Copies of production are a common leak.", "D"],
["Controls", "How do you design automated controls in Fusion to reduce SOX testing effort?", "**Why they ask:** automated controls cut audit cost.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I use configuration-based controls: matching tolerances, approval rules, duplicate checks, cross-validation rules, third-party control accounts and period statuses. Access controls come from SoD rules and role design. If change management controls are strong, auditors can test automated controls once by reviewing configuration, a benchmarking approach, instead of sampling transactions every year. Configuration changes keep change management evidence. And continuous controls monitoring watches transactions and configuration changes. The result is fewer manual controls and faster audits.\"\n\nTip: Explain benchmarking. It's the reason automated controls save money.", "D"],
["Banking", "How do you design payment controls to prevent fraud?", "**Why they ask:** payment fraud costs companies millions, and controls are part of the ERP design.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I start with segregation of duties: the person who changes supplier bank details can't enter invoices or release payments. Every bank account change is verified with a callback to a known contact and approved. Payment process requests above a threshold, or all of them, need approval before files are sent. Checks use positive pay, and where available a payee name check. Payment files are encrypted and signed, transmitted automatically, and nobody can edit them by hand. Then monitoring: a report of first payments to new bank accounts and payments outside normal patterns, reviewed before release.\"\n\nTip: Lead with bank account changes. That's where most real fraud happens.", "D"],
["Accounts Payable", "How would you architect an AP automation program end to end?", "**Why they ask:** an architect is expected to see the whole program, from how invoices arrive to how success is measured.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I design it in layers, following the invoice. Inbound: e-invoicing through EDI, PEPPOL or the supplier portal first, IDR for PDFs, and paper scanning only as a last resort. Validation: PO matching, tolerances, duplicate detection and tax validation. Approval: rules-based, with matched invoices auto-approved. Exceptions: AI agents that suggest how to resolve holds and answer supplier status questions. Payments: timing tuned between early-pay discounts and cash position, plus virtual cards where suppliers accept them. Analytics across all of it: touchless rate, cycle time, cost per invoice. And a supplier enablement program, because the technology only works if suppliers use the right channel.\"\n\nTip: Name supplier enablement as its own workstream. Many automation programs fail because suppliers keep emailing PDFs.", "D"],
["Revenue", "How would you architect revenue recognition for a company with products, services and subscriptions?", "**Why they ask:** an architect-level question. They want a design that separates billing from revenue timing.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I'd let each business process use the system built for it, and have all of them feed one revenue engine. Product orders come through Order Management, subscriptions through Subscription Management, and professional services through Project Billing. All of them send source documents into Revenue Management, which builds the contracts and performance obligations. Finance owns the standalone selling price tables and updates them each quarter. Billing stays in AR through AutoInvoice and follows the contract terms, independent of when revenue is recognized. Accounting gives us contract assets, contract liabilities and revenue, and I'd build a monthly deferred revenue roll-forward and the remaining performance obligations report for the disclosures.\"\n\n- **Sources:** OM, Subscription Management, Project Billing.\n- **Engine:** Revenue Management for contracts, obligations, SSP allocation.\n- **Billing:** AR, separate from revenue timing.\n- **Reporting:** deferred revenue roll-forward, RPO disclosure.\n\nTip: Say \"billing and revenue are separate\" early. It's the key design idea.", "D"],
["Fixed Assets", "How would you architect asset accounting for a capital-intensive company with projects?", "**Why they ask:** an architect question. They want the pieces and how they connect.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I'd build it in layers. Capital projects run in Fusion Projects, with capitalization rules for how costs are grouped into assets. Assets holds a corporate book and tax books, plus an IFRS book if needed. Physical tracking, like tags and locations, comes from Maintenance or a third-party tool. Leased assets go through Lease Accounting with ROU assets. Impairment and revaluation are defined processes, not one-offs. If the business tracks components, I'd integrate with Maintenance work orders. The close has a set rhythm: depreciation runs, a CIP aging review so finished projects are capitalized, and reconciliation of every book to GL.\"\n\nTip: Mention the CIP aging review. Uncapitalized finished projects are the most common audit finding here.", "D"],
["Testing", "How do you design test automation for an Oracle Cloud Finance landscape?", "**Why they ask:** quarterly updates make automation essential.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Scope covers the top end-to-end flows, P2P, O2C and record-to-report, plus key reports and integrations. Tools are Oracle's or third-party ones like Opkey, Worksoft or Tricentis that support Redwood and classic UIs, plus API-level tests for integrations. Test data is reusable and survives refreshes. Tests run every quarterly update, after configuration changes, and before major releases. Scripts are maintained like code and updated when processes change. And pass/fail dashboards support go/no-go decisions.\"\n\nTip: Mention test data that survives refreshes. It's what breaks most automation.", "D"],
["Roadmap", "How do you build a 3-year Finance systems roadmap?", "**Why they ask:** architects shape the future, not just the current project.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I start with the current state: systems, pain points, KPIs and technical debt. Then the business strategy: growth, M&A, new markets, and the finance operating model, like shared services or a faster close. Capabilities are prioritized by value and dependency, for example AP automation, continuous close, revenue automation, FDI analytics and AI agents. Phasing includes quick wins in year one. Each initiative has costs and benefits. And finance leadership reviews the roadmap every quarter.\"\n\nTip: Mention quick wins in year one. They keep funding for years two and three.", "B"],
["Behavioral", "Tell me about a time you had to say no to a senior executive.", "**Why they ask:** architects must protect the platform.\n\n**Answer (example):**\n\"A regional CFO wanted a custom invoice approval engine outside Fusion for their region. As architect, I had to protect the global template. I listened to the real need, approvals by project manager, then showed how Fusion approval rules could route by project manager using a project attribute, and estimated the custom engine's cost and upgrade risk. They agreed to the standard approach, and it went live with the region's rollout with no custom code.\"\n\nTip: Say no to the solution, yes to the need. That's the message.", "B"],
["Behavioral", "How do you stay current with Oracle Cloud changes?", "**Why they ask:** Oracle changes every quarter.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I read the quarterly release readiness for Financials, SCM, EPM and OIC. I follow Oracle Cloud Customer Connect, Oracle blogs and product roadmaps. I keep certifications current, like the Financials implementation certification and OCI AI or architect certifications. I try new features hands-on in a sandbox, like AI agents and Redwood pages. And I learn from peers in user groups like OAUG and Quest.\"\n\nTip: Name something new you tried recently. It proves you do it.", "B"],
["Reporting", "How would you design Smart View and Essbase usage for a finance team?", "**Why they ask:** Smart View is finance's favorite tool.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"The GL balances cube is created per chart of accounts and calendar and updated when journals post. Smart View gives ad-hoc queries and formatted Excel reports linked to live data. Member names and aliases come from the hierarchies, so I design trees with user-friendly names. For performance, users avoid giant queries, select members carefully and suppress missing rows. A shared library of report templates and training for power users keeps it governed. And drill-down to journals keeps the audit trail.\"\n\nTip: Mention designing hierarchies for readable names. Users judge the tool by it.", "D"],
["Architecture", "What are the most common reasons Oracle Finance implementations fail, and how do you prevent them?", "**Why they ask:** experience shows in knowing what goes wrong.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Poor data quality, prevented by early profiling, cleansing and multiple mocks. Over-customization, prevented by a 'standard first' principle and a design authority. Weak business ownership, prevented by engaged process owners with decision rights. Underestimated integrations, prevented by an integration inventory and building them early. Not enough testing, prevented by realistic SIT, UAT and a parallel close. Neglected change management, prevented by role-based training and super users. And unclear scope, prevented by strong change control.\"\n\nTip: Give one example where you saw one of these and fixed it.", "B"],
["Localization", "How do you handle country localizations in a global Finance template?", "**Why they ask:** global templates live or die on localization.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"The global template covers common processes, and a localization layer per country covers tax, e-invoicing, statutory reports, bank formats, payment methods and legal numbering. I use Oracle's localizations where they exist and partners for gaps. Each country rollout has a checklist built with local finance, tax and legal. Local variants are documented and kept to a minimum. And I watch regulatory changes, like e-invoicing mandates, and put them into the roadmap.\"\n\nTip: Mention e-invoicing mandates. They're the biggest localization driver now.", "D"],
["Integration", "How would you design supplier and customer master data management across systems?", "**Why they ask:** master data problems spread everywhere.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"One system of record per domain, for example Fusion for suppliers, CRM for prospects, and Fusion for billing accounts. Golden record rules, duplicate checks and enrichment from sources like D&B. Updates flow one way, from the master to consumers through events, and I avoid two-way editing. Governance includes data stewards, approval workflows like supplier onboarding, and data quality KPIs. And if there are many systems and complex hierarchies, I'd consider an MDM tool.\"\n\nTip: Say \"avoid two-way editing\". It's the source of most sync problems.", "D"],
["Architecture", "What is your approach to reducing technical debt in an existing Oracle Finance landscape?", "**Why they ask:** most clients have years of build-up.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I inventory customizations, extensions, reports and integrations with their usage, cost and risk. I retire what isn't used and replace custom solutions with new standard features, checking every release. I modernize fragile point-to-point integrations onto OIC with events. I simplify security roles and approval rules. I fund debt reduction in the roadmap, for example 20% of capacity. And I track metrics like the number of custom objects and incidents caused by extensions.\"\n\nTip: Mention reserving capacity for it. Debt reduction without budget never happens.", "D"],
["Behavioral", "How do you mentor lead consultants to think like architects?", "**Why they ask:** architects grow other architects.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I ask 'what else does this affect?' on every design. I have them write decision records for their choices and review them together. I bring them into non-functional, integration and security discussions. I let them present designs to the design authority. And I share reading, Oracle documentation and architecture patterns, plus post-mortems from past projects.\"\n\nTip: Mention \"what else does this affect?\". It's the habit that makes an architect.", "B"],
["Architecture", "How do you design for upgrade-safety on Oracle SaaS?", "**Why they ask:** upgrades every quarter mean fragile designs break often.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I use only supported extension points: public REST APIs, BIP, Visual Builder, OIC and flexfields. In BIP I don't depend on undocumented tables where views exist, and I never use screen scraping. Extensions and integrations have regression automation. I track deprecations in release notes, like classic pages being replaced by Redwood. And I keep personalizations minimal and documented.\"\n\nTip: Mention tracking deprecations. It's how you see breaks coming.", "B"],
["Behavioral", "Walk me through a solution you architected end to end.", "**Why they ask:** the main architect interview question.\n\n**Answer (structure, three to four minutes):**\n\"Start with the business problem and goals, for example 25 entities on four legacy systems and a 12-day close. Then the constraints: timeline, budget, regulations and the integration landscape. Then the solution: enterprise structures, modules, EPM, integration platform, data strategy, security and reporting, with one simple diagram. Then two or three key decisions and their trade-offs. Then delivery: phases, your role and the team. Then outcomes, like close days, cost per invoice and audit results. And lessons learned.\"\n\nTip: Practice drawing the diagram on a whiteboard. Many interviews ask for it live.", "B"]
]);

// Added Oct 2026: Fusion platform, Redwood and AI Agent Studio architecture questions.
QB["ora-arch-fin"] = (QB["ora-arch-fin"] || []).concat([
["AI Agent Studio", "Explain the architecture of an AI agent in Oracle AI Agent Studio.", "**Why they ask:** a technical architecture question for anyone building agents.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"An agent is instructions, topics, tools and an LLM, running natively in Fusion on OCI. Tools connect it to data and actions: Business Object tools for Fusion APIs, Document tools for retrieval over documents including external repositories, External REST and connectors, MCP, Email, Deep Link and User Session. Workflows add deterministic steps through nodes, like business object calls, code, email triggers, vector reads and writes, human approval and agent nodes, and they can be chained. Security uses identity propagation, so the agent acts with the user's Fusion roles, and a credential store holds keys for outside services. There's a monitoring dashboard for sessions, latency, errors and tokens, plus tracing and evaluations. Agents can be called from Fusion pages, Teams or external apps, and A2A and MCP connect them to agents and tools from other vendors.\"\n\nTip: Organize it as agent, tools, workflows, security, observability. It's easy to follow.", "D"],
["AI Agent Studio", "When would you use a workflow agent instead of a free-form conversational agent?", "**Why they ask:** choosing the right agent type is a key design decision.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I use a workflow when the steps are known and the result must be consistent and auditable, like routing invoice exceptions, preparing accruals or checking a new supplier. I add agent nodes only where judgment or interpretation is needed. I use a conversational agent for open questions, like policy Q&A or status inquiries. In finance, I lean toward workflows with human approval nodes for anything that changes financial data.\"\n\nTip: Say \"agent nodes only where judgment is needed\". It shows a controlled design mindset.", "D"],
["AI Agent Studio", "How do RAG and document tools work in AI Agent Studio, and what should you watch for?", "**Why they ask:** grounded answers depend on document quality.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Documents, like policies, contracts and procedures, are split into chunks and embedded. When someone asks a question, the agent retrieves the relevant chunks and uses them to ground its answer. Oracle supports multimodal content and external repositories like SharePoint. What I watch: document quality and versions, because a stale policy gives a confident wrong answer; access control on documents; citing sources with deep links; testing with tricky questions; and refreshing the index when documents change.\"\n\nTip: Lead the risks with stale documents. It's the most common real failure.", "D"],
["AI Agent Studio", "How would you connect a Fusion agent to non-Oracle systems?", "**Why they ask:** agents rarely live in one system.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"For systems with APIs, like bank portals, tax engines or CRM, I use External REST or connector tools. Where the system exposes an MCP server, I use MCP tools. To work with agents on other platforms, A2A. For complex orchestration and transformations, OIC behind the scenes. Secrets go in the credential store, service accounts get least privilege, and failures are monitored.\"\n\nTip: Mention least-privilege service accounts. Security reviewers will ask.", "D"],
["AI Agent Studio", "How do you choose the LLM for an agent?", "**Why they ask:** model choice affects quality, speed and cost.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Oracle provides Fusion-optimized models, and premium models from several providers can be selected. I choose by task: reasoning-heavy exceptions need a stronger model; simple lookups don't. Then latency, cost, since premium models are charged by tokens, and any data residency requirements. I evaluate the candidates on the same evaluation set before deciding, and re-evaluate when models change.\"\n\nTip: Say you test on the same evaluation set. It shows you decide with evidence.", "D"],
["Redwood", "Explain the Redwood extension architecture vs classic Fusion customization.", "**Why they ask:** extension skills are changing with Redwood.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Classic pages are ADF, extended with Page Composer and sandboxes, Application Composer in some areas, and Groovy. Redwood pages are built on Oracle JET and Visual Builder, and extended with Visual Builder Studio: page extensions for fields, layouts and rules, stored in Git and deployed through a pipeline. Flexfields are still how we add data. APIs and FBDI don't change, though deep-link formats can. As an architect, I plan for classic personalizations to retire and avoid fragile CSS or DOM tweaks, using Redwood design tokens and supported extension points instead.\"\n\nTip: Mention Git and pipelines. Redwood extensions are code, and that changes how teams work.", "D"],
["Redwood", "How do you build a custom Redwood-style application for Finance users?", "**Why they ask:** some gaps need a custom app.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I use Visual Builder Studio to build a Redwood app on Fusion REST APIs and Redwood components, so it looks native. It can be hosted as an extension or a standalone Visual Builder app, with SSO through Fusion identity. I'd use it for gaps like a custom reconciliation workbench or a guided intake form. And it goes into the extension inventory, the CI/CD pipeline and regression testing like any other extension.\"\n\nTip: Give a concrete gap you'd fill. It shows judgment about when to build.", "D"],
["Fusion Platform", "What changed in Fusion integration and data options recently that an architect should know?", "**Why they ask:** to see if your integration knowledge is current.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Business events with OIC now support event-driven integration. REST APIs keep expanding across modules, while FBDI stays for bulk loads. BICC handles bulk extracts, and Fusion Data Intelligence is the analytics platform. AI Agent Studio tools reuse the same business objects and APIs. And Redwood page extensions are done in Visual Builder Studio. So I design new integrations around APIs and events, and keep FBDI for volume.\"\n\nTip: End with your design rule. It turns a list into a position.", "D"],
["Architecture", "How would you redesign an AP process end to end using Fusion AI capabilities?", "**Why they ask:** a current design question combining AP and AI.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Intake through e-invoicing and Intelligent Document Recognition for PDFs, with an agent handling supplier email questions. Validation with matching, tolerances and duplicate checks. Exceptions handled by a workflow agent that explains holds and routes them to owners with recommended actions. Rule-based approval, with matched invoices approved automatically. Optimized payment runs, and an agent answering payment status queries. Controls: human approval for financial changes, audit traces and SoD. And KPIs: touchless rate, days on hold and cost per invoice.\"\n\nTip: Name the KPIs. They show the redesign is measurable.", "D"],
["Accounting", "How do you design SLA rules for a new revenue stream without disrupting existing accounting?", "**Why they ask:** changes to live accounting are risky. They want a safe method.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"I identify the event classes and sources the new stream touches. I copy and version the accounting method, and add new journal line rules and account derivation rules with conditions that only fire for the new stream, so existing transactions aren't affected. I use supporting references to report the new stream. I test with an accounting matrix that includes reversals and adjustments. Then I activate it at the start of a period and reconcile the first close carefully.\"\n\nTip: Say \"conditions that only fire for the new stream\". That's how you avoid disrupting existing accounting.", "D"],
["Architecture", "What does an \"AI-ready\" Finance platform need?", "**Why they ask:** a forward-looking architecture question.\n\n**Answer:**\n\"Clean master data: suppliers, customers, the chart of accounts and hierarchies. Current releases and Redwood adoption, because AI features arrive there first. Documented APIs and events, with minimal custom code. A tight, well-understood security model, since agents inherit it. Governance: AI use-case intake, evaluation, monitoring and audit involvement. And a data platform like Fusion Data Intelligence for analytics and grounding.\"\n\nTip: Lead with clean data. AI on bad data just makes faster mistakes.", "D"]
]);
